"""ELF/linker assessments with independent field arithmetic and artifact controls."""
ROWS=r'''
01.a|Write a bounded ELF64 little-endian inspection tool for original object/executable/shared artifacts, reporting header, section, program-header and symbol entries. Reject short headers, overflowing count-times-entry extents and unsupported classes without dereferencing file bytes as native structs.|A valid magic number makes subsequent file offsets trustworthy.|Every table extent must be checked against file length before decoding; unaligned casts and host layout assumptions are unnecessary. Unsupported extended counts must reject explicitly or be implemented correctly.|Add ELF32 or big-endian decoding while retaining the same checked byte-reader contract.
01.b|For a synthetic PT_LOAD with offset0x1000, vaddr0x400000, filesz0x30, memsz0x50, derive mapped initialized and zero-fill ranges. Compare with a real original ELF and its section-to-segment mapping.|The section table alone specifies what the loader maps.|The declared segment contributes0x30 file-backed bytes and0x20 zero-filled bytes; sections and segments answer different questions. Filesz greater than memsz must reject.|Remove the section table from an otherwise supported original executable and explain which loader facts remain available.
01.c|Build one source as ET_REL, executable and shared object; inspect entry, undefined symbols, relocation tables and program headers. Classify each artifact before any execution attempt.|All files named .o or .so have identical loading responsibilities.|File extension is not authoritative; object type and metadata establish stage-specific meaning. ET_REL addresses cannot simply be treated as process addresses.|Add static PIE and explain why ET_DYN alone does not distinguish every executable from every shared library.
02.a|Create separate local, global, weak, undefined and tentative-definition examples, using explicit GCC extension/flag labels for weak and common behavior. Explain nm/readelf output and default -fno-common separately from ISO C definitions.|Identical symbol spelling implies one entity and common is a C storage class.|Local names can coexist; weak/common are object/tool rules, while source declarations retain C constraints. Modern defaults must be recorded rather than assumed.|Change tentative definitions to explicit initialized definitions and predict which formerly merged cases become conflicts.
02.b|Construct main -> a -> b across two objects and an archive; maintain the unresolved/defined symbol sets after each input. Add a strong definition competing with a weak one and compare the chosen origin in the link map.|The linker searches all possible definitions symmetrically and chooses arbitrarily.|Input order, extraction and binding rules explain the selected definition; a trace must identify the supplying object rather than only the final value.|Put the competing definition in an unextracted archive member and redo the trace.
02.c|Give two original TUs an accidental nonstatic helper named helper and retain the duplicate-definition diagnostic; repair ownership with internal linkage. Separately restrict shared-library dynamic exports and inspect the actual exported set.|Suppressing duplicate-definition errors repairs conflicting ownership.|The repair gives each internal helper the intended scope; dynamic visibility and C internal linkage are related but distinct controls.|Move one helper into the public API and define a single authoritative implementation/versioned contract.
03.a|Create a three-member archive where only one member is initially needed. Inspect members and symbol index; map each exported symbol to a member and preserve the archive command and ordering.|A static archive is already a fully linked executable component.|The archive groups relocatable members and an index; it does not resolve all internal references or establish final inclusion.|Rebuild the archive after removing a stale member and prove the old object cannot remain silently selected.
03.b|Use main calling a(), archive A member a calling b(), and archive B member b returning42. Predict main,A,B and main,B,A before linking, then explain each unresolved-set transition.|Archive ordering is cosmetic.|Demand-driven extraction explains the successful and unresolved cases under the declared linker; a missing compiler is not the intended order failure.|Create a backward reference from B to a second member of A and show why repeating or grouping A may be needed.
03.c|Compare normal extraction, a minimal group and whole-archive for a registration-only member plus unrelated dead code. Explain exactly which member requires forcing and measure included sections.|Whole-archive is a harmless universal fix for missing symbols.|Forcing all members can add initialization, code and conflicts; justify the smallest retention/extraction policy and separate section GC from member extraction.|Add a conflicting unused definition and diagnose why whole-archive newly fails while targeted extraction succeeds.
04.a|Compute the supplied S=4096,A=-4,P=4112 relocation and identify the corresponding symbolic/addend/place roles in one real original object. Distinguish an instruction-relative addend from the address of the next instruction.|A relocation is merely copying a symbol address into bytes.|The synthetic expression is-20; real relocation semantics must be selected by its actual type, field location and addend representation.|Use a REL object where the addend is stored in the relocated bytes and explain the changed decoder obligation.
04.b|For declared unsigned absolute32 and signed PC-relative32 models calculate boundary values, write independent little-endian encodings and compare one supported actual relocation of each kind.|Absolute and PC-relative relocations differ only in field size.|The formulas and signedness differ; a load-base change affects an absolute address differently from an intra-image relative displacement.|Move the target symbol to another load region and identify which relative assumptions no longer hold.
04.c|For a signed8 synthetic relocation compare-20 and236, whose truncated low byte is identical. Reject out-of-range values before encoding and construct a real large-displacement link test only within an original nonexecuted layout.|Truncating to the field width is a valid overflow policy.|-20 fits and236 does not; equal low bytes do not imply equal relocation meaning. Distinguish intended overflow diagnostics from unrelated link failure.|Change the relocation's signedness or code model and derive a new admissible interval before altering flags.
05.a|Compile the same address-taking source as non-PIE, PIE and shared PIC with consistent compile/link options. Compare runtime-relocation obligations and loaded-address assumptions without treating ET_DYN as sufficient evidence.|PIC, PIE and static linking are interchangeable properties.|Position independence, executable role and runtime linkage are separate dimensions; actual metadata and address formation substantiate each claim.|Build a supported static-PIE variant and locate its self-relocation owner rather than assuming no loader means no relocations.
05.b|Follow one local object address and one externally visible function reference through object disassembly, relocations, GOT/PLT entries and final linked code. Preserve symbolic correspondence across linker relaxation.|Every source address operation requires the same indirection sequence.|Visibility and binding can change indirection or relaxation; final code is the artifact being executed. Source-level operation count is not a dynamic instruction count.|Hide an export or change interposition policy and explain which optimization becomes valid.
05.c|Count relocation records and writable pages affected for two equivalent original images, then separately measure startup under stated cache/environment conditions. Report record count, timing and causal uncertainty distinctly.|More relocation records necessarily imply proportionally slower startup.|Static counts and dynamic costs are different observations; relocation type, loader behavior and shared pages affect cost. Equivalent behavior is required for comparison.|Change only eager/lazy binding and separate work moved to first call from work eliminated.
06.a|Load a trusted original plugin by absolute path and then by a controlled local search path; record its recursive dependencies and selected interpreter. Deliberately supply a missing dependency without altering system loader configuration.|Finding the first plugin proves its entire dependency closure is present.|Each dependency has its own resolution; failure must name the missing object. Local process environment changes do not establish universal search behavior.|Relocate the plugin and its dependency together and justify an origin-relative policy without private build paths.
06.b|Provide two original implementations of a toy function returning11 or22 and compare an explicitly controlled interposition experiment. Inspect GOT/PLT and binding logs; no third-party process is targeted.|A symbol's source definition uniquely fixes every dynamic call target.|Visibility, lookup scope and binding policy can redirect the toy call; numeric output alone does not identify the selected symbol origin.|Hide the symbol or bind it locally and explain which interposition expectation changes.
06.c|Create a plugin with a deliberately unresolved function used only on an uncalled branch. Compare RTLD_NOW and RTLD_LAZY in separate bounded child processes with a known-good plugin control.|Lazy mode postpones resolution of all symbols including data.|The declared loader may defer function resolution; data relocations and other loading work remain immediate. Unsupported lazy behavior must be reported, not guessed.|Replace the missing function reference with a data reference and predict the changed load-time outcome.
07.a|Extend the existing TLS test to record values and addresses for main and two threads before/after independent increments; inspect TLS sections and access sequences. Do not retain a pointer after its thread's lifetime.|Thread-local storage is one globally shared object with a special keyword.|Independent thread state and lifetime must both hold; the existing10/11 observations cover values but not every TLS model or lifetime case.|Load an original TLS-bearing plugin after threads already exist and identify additional loader/runtime responsibilities.
07.b|Put two original initialization hooks in an ordered toy executable and record hook/body/finalization events. Identify sections and the runtime loop that invokes them; do not assume order beyond the explicit construction.|The linker executes functions merely by placing pointers in an init array.|Metadata supplies addresses; startup/loader code performs calls. Recorded ordering must be tied to the selected runtime and link policy.|Link the same metadata into a custom-entry image that omits the walker and explain why no hook executes automatically.
07.c|Draw producer/consumer/lifetime chains for TLS templates, init arrays and fini arrays using the supplied runtime source passages and actual metadata. Mark uninspected runtime bodies explicitly instead of filling gaps from function names.|A section name is evidence that the required initialization has occurred.|Presence of metadata, an identified consumer and observed execution are separate evidence categories; missing consumer work remains open.|Retain only one initialization service in a custom runtime and specify all dependencies its callback contract permits.
08.a|Write an original linker script with FLASH[0x10000,0x10100) and RAM[0x20000,0x20080), explicit text/data/BSS sections and alignment. Reject an over-capacity object using ASSERT; do not execute the synthetic image.|A valid syntax script establishes a physically valid board memory map.|The map proves the declared model only; region overflow/alignment and orphan-section policy need explicit checks. No board mapping is inferred.|Add a second RAM bank and define placement when a section cannot fit contiguously in either bank.
08.b|Place16 initialized data bytes at RAM VMA0x20000 with FLASH LMA0x10080, plus24 BSS bytes. Derive source/destination half-open intervals and verify the load image contains initialization bytes but not24 BSS payload bytes.|VMA, LMA and file offset are interchangeable addresses.|Startup copies16 bytes from load to run location and zeros24 bytes; file offset is obtained from format mapping, not guessed from VMA.|Introduce alignment padding between load sections and recalculate the image extent and copy table.
08.c|Export data_start,data_end,data_load symbols and document the chosen compiler/linker C declaration convention. Use symbol values to construct checked counts under that implementation contract, avoiding dereferencing a linker absolute symbol as stored data.|A linker symbol necessarily denotes an ISO C allocated object containing its numeric value.|Address-valued linker symbols and ordinary objects differ; subtraction/provenance assumptions must be explicitly implementation-bound.|Use an explicit startup table of address/length records and validate each interval before initialization.
09.a|Put an indirect registration entry in an archive member with no ordinary reference. Compare KEEP alone, explicit extraction and section GC, recording both member inclusion and retained sections.|KEEP pulls every matching section out of every archive automatically.|An unextracted member supplies no input section for KEEP; extraction and section reachability must be diagnosed separately.|Replace the archive with directly linked objects and explain why the same KEEP rule now has a different effect.
09.b|Build a table of two original callbacks reached only through registration, retain it deliberately and prove an unrelated unused function is eliminated. Check table boundaries and callback ownership.|Taking an address in source always makes an entry reachable after linking.|A retained table must create actual relocation edges to the callbacks; source mention and compiler emission alone do not guarantee final reachability.|Store numeric offsets instead of relocation-bearing pointers and identify the lost automatic reachability evidence.
09.c|Diagnose a missing registration entry by tracing compiler emission, archive extraction, section GC and startup enumeration in order. Supply a deliberately broken case at each relevant stage.|Every missing function after link is fixed by disabling garbage collection.|The first absent artifact identifies the mechanism; broad retention can hide missing extraction or enumeration bugs.|Change to LTO and show how the compiler/linker boundary shifts the required evidence.
10.a|Use real map files and ASSERT to accept32 BSS bytes and reject128 against a64-byte budget, then add alignment and a second section. Independently compute the expected region end.|Summing source sizeof values always equals linked region occupancy.|Padding and placement affect the map; the rejection must be the budget assertion rather than another linker failure.|Include a reserved stack region and prove the combined static allocation cannot overlap it.
10.b|Create text, constant, initialized and zero-initialized objects, inspect actual section placement and account for retained versus optimized-away bytes. Separate ROM initialization bytes from RAM residency.|BSS is free because it occupies no ordinary data payload in the file.|Zero-fill still consumes runtime memory; initialized data often consumes both load-image and runtime storage. Classification follows actual retained sections.|Merge identical constants or enable section GC and explain the resulting resource delta without changing the correctness contract.
10.c|Compare ELF file size, raw load-image extent and runtime RAM for the same original layout, including headers/debug bytes and sparse gaps. State the metric before comparing configurations.|A smaller ELF necessarily means less flash and RAM.|Metadata/debug data and sparse encoding can change file size independently of load/runtime requirements.|Convert to a sparse address-bearing format and explain why its smaller file is not a smaller address span.
11.a|Define an original plugin ABI with a version/size header, one exported operation and hidden helpers. Reject a caller with an unsupported contract before interpreting later fields and inspect the dynamic symbol set.|Hiding helpers alone gives a stable semantic ABI.|Visibility reduces exposed names but layout, ownership, errors and behavior remain contracts; the size/version gate must prevent incompatible access.|Add an optional tail field while retaining an older consumer and specify precise default behavior.
11.b|Export two named toy interface versions and test old/new consumer combinations, including one deliberately missing required version. Separate symbol-resolution failure from changed numeric behavior.|A version label guarantees backward-compatible semantics.|Version availability is only one predicate; observable behavior and representation must satisfy the promised version contract.|Keep the version name unchanged while changing error semantics and design a compatibility test that catches it.
11.c|Change a public structure's layout and separately change an implementation behind an opaque handle. Classify source rebuild, old-binary linkage and semantic compatibility in a matrix; unsafe old-layout execution is excluded.|Recompiling successfully proves old binaries remain compatible.|Source, symbol and behavioral compatibility are independent; an unchanged symbol can conceal a layout break.|Change ownership of a returned pointer without changing its C type and identify the binary-compatible but semantically incompatible contract.
12.a|Define original sparse bytes at0x1000:{01,02} and0x1010:{FE,FF}; derive raw output under explicit zero-gap fill and an address-bearing Intel HEX output. Decode both independently before any use.|Raw conversion preserves load addresses without external metadata.|Raw bytes need a base/fill policy; address records preserve different information. The raw span is18bytes under the stated endpoints.|Use a nonzero gap-fill byte and show how checksum and image size accounting change.
12.b|Produce a stripped original executable and separate debug companion, record hashes/build identity, and resolve one symbol/address through the matching companion. Deliberately pair a different build's debug file and reject it.|Any debug file from the same source name matches the executable.|Artifact identity and address correspondence are required; stripping loses information that cannot be recovered from the raw image alone.|Re-link identical source at a changed base and explain why the old address correlation fails.
12.c|Reserve one checksum byte in an original16-byte image. Define coverage as the other15 bytes, patch exactly once, and verify both prepatch and postpatch manifests with an independent sum-mod256 model; keep the debug ELF immutable.|A checksum value is meaningful without specifying covered bytes and patch order.|The excluded checksum position prevents self-reference under this declared model; patching changes artifact identity and must not reuse a prepatch hash.|Include the checksum field in a zero-sum invariant and derive the different patch equation and verification procedure.
'''
def rows():
 out={}
 for line in ROWS.strip().splitlines():
  key,task,misconception,expected,variant=line.split('|');out['T09.'+key]=dict(task=task,misconception=misconception,expected=expected,variant=variant)
 return out
